description:
Here is a professional and comprehensive OSF project description suitable for your systematic review and diagnostic test accuracy meta-analysis:

---

This project is a systematic review and diagnostic test accuracy (DTA) meta-analysis evaluating the performance of machine learning and deep learning models for differentiating benign and malignant pulmonary nodules. Pulmonary nodules are frequently detected during thoracic imaging, particularly computed tomography (CT) screening, and accurate distinction between benign and malignant lesions is essential for early lung cancer diagnosis, treatment planning, and reduction of unnecessary invasive procedures. In recent years, artificial intelligence (AI), machine learning (ML), and deep learning (DL) techniques have increasingly been investigated as diagnostic support tools for pulmonary nodule classification. However, the reported diagnostic performance, methodological quality, and clinical applicability of these models vary substantially across studies.

The primary aim of this project is to systematically identify, critically evaluate, and quantitatively synthesize the available evidence regarding the diagnostic accuracy of ML and DL systems for pulmonary nodule classification. The review focuses on studies reporting diagnostic performance metrics such as sensitivity, specificity, area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC), likelihood ratios, diagnostic odds ratios, and confusion-matrix-derived outcomes. The study also examines methodological heterogeneity, dataset characteristics, validation strategies, model architectures, threshold definitions, and risk of bias.

A comprehensive literature search will be conducted across multiple electronic databases, including Web of Science, Scopus, PubMed, Embase, Europe PMC, Semantic Scholar, and other relevant sources. Eligible studies will include original research articles evaluating AI-based pulmonary nodule classification systems using imaging datasets. Studies focusing exclusively on segmentation, non-diagnostic tasks, or lacking diagnostic performance data will be excluded. Data extraction will include study design, imaging modality, sample size, machine learning architecture, validation methods, diagnostic metrics, and clinically relevant methodological characteristics.

Methodological quality and applicability concerns will be assessed using the QUADAS-2 framework. Where appropriate and clinically interpretable, pooled diagnostic accuracy analyses will be performed using random-effects models. Sensitivity, specificity, likelihood ratios, and diagnostic odds ratios will be synthesized quantitatively, while AUC values and heterogeneous outcomes may be summarized descriptively. Heterogeneity, small-study effects, and reporting transparency will also be explored.

The expected outcomes of this project include:

1. A comprehensive evidence synthesis of AI-based pulmonary nodule classification models;
2. Quantitative pooled estimates of diagnostic performance where appropriate;
3. Identification of methodological limitations and sources of bias in current studies;
4. Evaluation of the clinical readiness and generalizability of ML/DL systems;
5. Recommendations for future research, external validation, reporting standards, and clinical implementation.

This project aims to contribute to the growing field of AI-assisted lung cancer diagnostics by clarifying the current strengths, limitations, and translational potential of machine learning approaches for pulmonary nodule classification.
